      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the dough: combine the flour, 1 teaspoon of salt, 1 teaspoon of instant yeast, vegetable oil and 180ml warm water in a bowl. Mix until a soft dough forms, then knead for 5 minutes. Cover and leave to rest for 30 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oven to 240C (Fan 220C / Gas 9). Place 2 baking trays inside to heat up."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the topping: pulse the onion, garlic, tomato, red pepper and parsley in a food processor until very finely chopped but not a pure paste. Squeeze out excess liquid through a sieve or clean tea towel. Mix into the lamb mince along with all the spices, salt and black pepper."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the dough into 4 equal pieces. On a lightly floured surface, use the rolling pin to roll each piece into a very thin oval or round, roughly 30cm across."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Spread a thin, even layer of topping over each base, right to the edges. The layer should be no more than 3mm thick."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Working quickly, slide the lahmacun onto the hot baking trays. Bake for 8 to 10 minutes until the edges are golden and the topping is cooked through. Repeat with remaining pieces."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve immediately, rolled up with sliced tomato, red onion, parsley and a squeeze of lemon."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Topping is wet and does not cook through"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The tomato and vegetables release a lot of water when processed. Squeeze the mixture very firmly through a clean tea towel before mixing with the mince. The topping should feel barely moist, not wet."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Base is soft and doughy"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Roll the dough as thin as you can, and always bake on a preheated tray. A cold tray makes the base steam rather than crisp."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Edges burn before centre cooks"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Spread the topping all the way to the very edges so there is no bare dough exposed. Bare dough colours much faster than the covered areas."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Known across Turkey and into Lebanon, Syria and Armenia, "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "lahmacun-term"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "lahmacun"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is a street food staple sold from dedicated shops that do nothing else. The key is the oven temperature: Turkish lahmacun is cooked in a wood-fired oven at extreme heat in under two minutes. A domestic oven at maximum heat gets close enough. The result should be crisp enough to roll without cracking and soft enough to eat without a knife."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
